      "background": [
        {
          "fact": "In embroidery, a DST file represents machine stitches rather than a pixel image.",
          "source": "https://wilcom.com/resources/blog/compare-emb-and-dst"
        },
        {
          "fact": "Its stitch coordinates tell an embroidery machine how to move and sew.",
          "source": "https://wilcom.com/resources/blog/compare-emb-and-dst"
        },
        {
          "fact": "The design can include changes in thread and other machine actions.",
          "source": "https://wilcom.com/resources/blog/compare-emb-and-dst"
        },
        {
          "fact": "Ordinary DST files do not preserve a thread palette; pyembroidery also documents an extended-header variant with color information.",
          "source": "https://github.com/EmbroidePy/pyembroidery"
        },
        {
          "fact": "Enlarging a basic DST design spreads existing stitches without increasing their count.",
          "source": "https://wilcom.com/resources/blog/compare-emb-and-dst"
        },
